// ADR-0076 (#817) Phase 3: a minimal directly-nested handle pair for two
// DIFFERENT effects (`handle { handle { both() } with A {...} } with B
// {...}`), with no wrapper-function indirection at all -- the general
// shape edp_has_unsafe_construct's nested-EHandle relaxation targets, kept
// separate from fixtures/effect_handle_multi_effect_row_nested.vibe (which
// combines this with a multi-effect row AND a wrapper function, closer to
// a real-world "helper discharges one effect, caller discharges another"
// pattern).
//
// Before this relaxation, `B`'s handle site here had a body that is
// directly `handle { both() } with A {...}` -- an `EHandle` node
// edp_has_unsafe_construct unconditionally rejected, so only `A` (the
// innermost effect) could ever migrate; `B` was always excluded, no
// matter how simple or safe its own contribution. edp_has_unsafe_construct
// now recurses into a nested handle for a DIFFERENT effect (checking its
// body and, since a handler arm's tail call is always `resume(...)`, its
// arms too -- a bare `resume` call is recognized as safe rather than an
// unrecognized, therefore-unsafe, callee) instead of rejecting it outright.
// `edp_rewrite_needing_body`/`edp_rewrite_handle_body` mirror the same
// traversal so whatever eligibility allows also gets correctly rewritten.
//
// Verified (via a temporary migration-plan probe during development,
// confirming both `A` and `B` genuinely produce and apply a migration
// plan, not merely "doesn't crash") that BOTH effects migrate to
// evidence-dict here: `both`'s signature gains both dicts (`B`'s
// prepended last, since `B`'s migration runs after `A`'s and re-derives
// the current, already-`A`-migrated signature/call site fresh -- the
// two-phase design's "decide all eligibility upfront against the
// unmutated tree, then apply sequentially against the live tree" split
// is exactly what makes this composition safe). No replay/frontier
// machinery is used for either effect: `compute`'s body runs exactly once.
//
//   x == 3 (perform A::Get, resolved via A's evidence dict)
//   y == 4 (perform B::Get, resolved via B's evidence dict)
//   both() == x + y == 7
//   main() == 7
effect A {
  Get -> Int
}

effect B {
  Get -> Int
}

let both = () -> Int with A + B {
  let x = perform A::Get
  let y = perform B::Get
  x + y
}

let compute = () -> Int {
  handle {
    handle {
      both()
    } with {
      A::Get => resume(3)
    }
  } with {
    B::Get => resume(4)
  }
}

export let main = () -> Int {
  compute()
}

// #1571: the expectation lives here, checked by `inspect` and updatable
// with `vibe test --update`, instead of in a `__DATA__` tail the gate has
// to `sed` off and compare in shell.
test "effect handle multi effect nested handles" {
  inspect(main(), "7")
}
